FINDINGS = [   # (id, section, text, cites)
 ("F1", "Background", "The chapter's first claim, that a Bitcoin payment authorizes only a specific value to a specific recipient and cannot be forged, was run: a signed payment of 222 bytes held a signature of 71 bytes and the 33-byte public key and neither the private key nor its WIF text; the same payment with a swapped recipient script or with the first output lowered from 1.5 to 1 coin was refused with mempool-script-verify-flag-failed; and a replay after confirmation was refused with -27, so the claim stands for the default signature type SIGHASH_ALL, which the wallet used (byte 01), while BIP 143 says hashOutputs commits to all outputs only when the type is neither SINGLE nor NONE (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Lau and Wuille, 2016); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Nakamoto, 2008).", ["P01", "P18", "P48", "P51"]),
 ("F2", "Contemporary developments", "Changed default: the chapter says that no encryption is required for Bitcoin traffic and chapter 10 says that Bitcoin Core communicates entirely in the clear at the time of writing, but BIP 324 defines an encrypted version 2 transport, release 26.0 added it switched off, release 27.0 enabled it by default and the help text of 31.1 reads -v2transport Support v2 transport (default: 1); two private nodes of 31.1 reported the transport v2 with the same 64-digit session id, and a node started with -v2transport=0 connected as v1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Mehta et al., 2019); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026).", ["P01", "P08", "P26", "P44", "P77", "P47"]),
 ("F3", "Contemporary developments", "Changed state of the root of trust: the book says that the genesis block is the root of trust and that the only thing to be explicitly trusted is a fully validated blockchain; the genesis hash is indeed typed into chainparams.cpp and asserted, but Bitcoin Core 31.1 also ships defaultAssumeValid, block 938,343 mined on 2026-02-25, 33,237 blocks below the tip of 971,580 on the day of the fetch, and the help text says the node assumes that block and its ancestors are valid and potentially skips their script verification unless started with -assumevalid=0 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026); (Mempool Space, 2026).", ["P01", "P35", "P47", "P76", "P74", "P73"]),
 ("F4", "Contemporary developments", "Erratum of the source file: the AsciiDoc of the chapter begins with the anchor [[ch11]] although the chapter is number 13, while the chapters 3, 4 and 5 carry anchors such as ch03_bitcoin_client, ch04_keys_addresses and ch05_wallets, so the anchor is a left-over of the numbering of an earlier edition; the book's own list of errata, Appendix B, does not mention it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).", ["P01", "P11"]),
 ("F5", "Contemporary developments", "Changed default for wallets: the book speaks of a wallet that contains keys and can be backed up like any file, but since release 23.0 new wallets are descriptor wallets, release 30.0 can no longer create or load the Berkeley DB legacy wallets, which can only be migrated, and the boxed warning of the chapter, that more than the seed may be needed to spend, is exactly the reason for descriptors: a wallet holding one key of a 2-of-3 knew nothing of the multisignature address, a wallet given the descriptor again found the 2.0 coins, and the same keys in another order under multi found 0.0 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2022); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025); (Wuille and Chow, 2021).", ["P01", "P43", "P45", "P21", "P36"]),
 ("F6", "Contemporary developments", "Changed behaviour of backups: encryptwallet in Bitcoin Core 31.1 answers wallet encrypted; The keypool has been flushed and a new HD seed was generated, so a backup made before the encryption cannot recover coins received by the new seed, and the managing-wallets document and the source both say to make a new backup at once; in one run the 32 key bytes found in the wallet file before were not found after, which does not exclude traces elsewhere on the disk (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026).", ["P31", "P39"]),
 ("F7", "Contemporary developments", "The encryption of the wallet behaves as documented: a locked wallet showed its balance and refused to send with error -13, a wrong or changed-away passphrase gave -14, an unlock timeout of 3 seconds ended exactly when the key was dropped, and the document states that there is no way to recover a passphrase; the book's advice not to lose the keys by over-protecting them is the same lesson (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).", ["P31", "P39", "P01"]),
 ("F8", "Contemporary developments", "A stale backup can lose money when the keypool is small: on a node with -keypool=3 the restored old backup held 1.0 coin of 3.0 because 2.0 coins went to the tenth address, outside the range 0 to 2; the help text of 31.1 warns about this and gives the default size 1000, and Bitcoin.org advises regular backups in many secure locations (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin.org, 2026).", ["P47", "P32", "P75"]),
 ("F9", "Comparative analysis", "Cold storage with a watch-only wallet and a PSBT worked on test nodes: the hot wallet had private keys disabled and could not sign (complete false, error -4 when asked for private descriptors), the cold node with 0 blocks and 0 connections signed the 528-character PSBT, and the hot node finalized and broadcast it; the roles are those of BIP 174, a second node on the same machine is not an air gap, and Wikipedia notes that Stuxnet-like malware was designed to cross air gaps on removable media (Chow, 2017); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2018); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026).", ["P19", "P42", "P37", "P72"]),
 ("F10", "Comparative analysis", "The book expects hardware signing devices to become the predominant storage; Bitcoin Core 31.1 supports external signers with -signer=<cmd>, BIP 388 defines wallet policies that a device registers so that it can show addresses on its own screen, and the Bitcoin Wiki still warns that malware could swap the recipient's address, so the device protects the key and not the intention; no device was run (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Ingala, 2023); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P01", "P40", "P24", "P60"]),
 ("F11", "Comparative analysis", "The advice to print a seed is now qualified: the Bitcoin Wiki calls the paper wallet, a printed single key, an obsolete and unsafe method of 2011 to 2016 because printers keep files, and the Bitcoin Wiki on securing a wallet puts the seed on paper in a safe place among several; the brainwallet is worse, since four words from a list of 2,048 give 2 to the power 44 possibilities, 17.6 seconds at an assumed trillion guesses per second, and BIP 38 answers with scrypt (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Caldwell and Voisine, 2012).", ["P01", "P61", "P59", "P65", "P14"]),
 ("F12", "Comparative analysis", "The seed arithmetic was recomputed against published vectors: the BIP 39 entropy of 16 zero bytes gives abandon eleven times and about, the seed with the passphrase TREZOR and 2048 PBKDF2 rounds equals the Trezor vector and its master xprv, and BIP 32's test vector 1 gives the printed xprv9s21ZrQH143K3QTDL4LX...; Bitcoin Core does not use words, as none of seven saved Core files mentions a mnemonic, while the book's chapter 5 lists BIP 39 and SLIP39 (Palatinus et al., 2013); (Trezor developers, 2026); (Wuille, 2012); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).", ["P12", "P27", "P28", "P13", "P02"]),
 ("F13", "Comparative analysis", "The wallet encryption of Bitcoin Core is old and simple: AES-256-CBC with a 32-byte key, an 8-byte salt, a 16-byte IV and 25000 derivation rounds that the source says took just under 0.1 seconds on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M, 12.2 times the 2048 rounds of BIP 39; RFC 8018 explains the reason for iterating, and the strength of the encryption is therefore mainly the strength of the passphrase (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Moriarty et al., 2017); (Palatinus et al., 2013).", ["P29", "P30", "P53", "P12"]),
 ("F14", "Comparative analysis", "Multisignature works as the chapter says: a 2-of-3 wallet needed two signers (one signature left it incomplete, two made it complete in 146 virtual bytes), the loss of one key left the funds spendable, the loss of two did not, sortedmulti gave the same address for any order of keys while multi gave different ones, and the same payment cost 110 virtual bytes with one key, 36 fewer; BIP 67 sorts the keys lexicographically (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Kerin et al., 2015); (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P01", "P15", "P36", "P66"]),
 ("F15", "Comparative analysis", "The odds depend on what is feared: with each key lost at 0.1 and stolen at 0.05 independently, an assumption, 2-of-3 is stuck at 0.028 and robbed at 0.00725 against 0.1 and 0.05 for one key, while 1-of-3 is stuck at 0.001 and robbed at 0.14263, and at a loss chance of 0.5 the 2-of-3 equals the single key; real keys are not independent when they share a drawer (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P01", "P66"]),
 ("F16", "Comparative analysis", "The chapter does not mention secret sharing: Shamir's paper of November 1979 defines a (k, n) threshold scheme in which k minus 1 pieces leave the secret completely undetermined, the library gave three shares of which any two recovered the secret and over the field 257 one share fitted 257 secrets, BIP 93 (codex32, status Draft) splits a seed into up to 31 shares, and the book's chapter 5 lists SLIP39 (Shamir, 1979); (Olsson Curr et al., 2023); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Wikipedia contributors, 2026).", ["P52", "P25", "P02", "P69", "P70"]),
 ("F17", "Comparative analysis", "The story of the education project that in July 2011 lost almost 7,000 bitcoin with its encryption keys is told only by the book among the 77 saved sources, with no name; it is a lesson and not a confirmed event here, and the Bitcoin Wiki gives the general advice that failed backups and mistyped letters have lost coins (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P01", "P59"]),
 ("F18", "Comparative analysis", "The figures of the Mt. Gox loss differ by source and should be quoted with their source: Wikipedia reports a leaked document of 744,408 bitcoins lost in a theft undetected for years, an earlier count of 850,000 reduced to 650,000 by 199,999.99 found in an old wallet, and a share of over 70% of all transactions by early 2014, while the Bitcoin Wiki says 640 thousand; the book's remark about early exchanges with a single hot wallet fits the design (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).", ["P67", "P63", "P01"]),
 ("F19", "Comparative analysis", "Custody that was called secured also failed: Bitfinex partnered with BitGo in 2015 for secured wallets, lost 1,500 bitcoins in May 2015, and in August 2016 lost 119,756 bitcoin from users' segregated wallets, after which the price fell by 20%; the page does not give the technical cause, so no cause is claimed here (Wikipedia contributors, 2026).", ["P71"]),
 ("F20", "Comparative analysis", "Physical bitcoin was a real product and a regulatory case: Casascius coins carried a private key under a tamper-evident hologram, were sold until 26 November 2013 in 1, 10, 25, 100 and 1000 BTC sizes, and FinCEN told the maker that minting physical bitcoins made him a money transmitter (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P62"]),
 ("F21", "Comparative analysis", "The Trezor is the first device listed in the Bitcoin Wiki's chronological list of commercial hardware wallets; its PIN waits double and the device wipes after 16 failed attempts, 32,767 seconds of waiting in all, and the Trezor Safe 5 of 14 June 2024 has an EAL 6+ secure element, while the Safe 7 of 21 October 2025 announces an open secure element (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P64", "P60"]),
 ("F22", "Contemporary developments", "A trap in survivability: an heir path or(owner, and(heir, older 10)) behaved as designed on the test chain (heir refused at 1 and 9 confirmations, accepted at 10, owner accepted at once), but BIP 68 reads only 16 bits of the sequence and Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepted older(65536), with which the heir could spend after 1 confirmation, no wait at all; the longest honest wait is 65,535 blocks (about 455 days) or 65,535 units of 512 seconds (388 days) (Friedenbach et al., 2015); (BtcDrak et al., 2015); (Bitcoin Core, 2026).", ["P16", "P17"]),
 ("F23", "Comparative analysis", "The release of the software was verified as far as it can be without keys: the SHA-256 of the 90,293,352-byte tarball equals its line among the 28 in SHA256SUMS, but gpg could call none of the 11 signatures of SHA256SUMS.asc good with an empty keyring (exit 2), so the hash proves only that the file is the one listed; the Guix instructions describe the reproducible build that lets many builders agree on the bytes (Bitcoin Core release signers, 2026); (Bitcoin Core, 2026).", ["P49", "P50", "P41"]),
 ("F24", "Conclusion", "What was not executed is recorded with its reason: a hardware device, a true air gap, paper and metal backups, an exchange breach, estate planning with a lawyer, mainnet funds, Shamir in a wallet, BIP 39 recovery in Core, a good gpg verification and BIP 324 on the wire; every regtest value is a single-run value (Bitcoin Core, 2026); (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).", ["P47", "P01"]),
 ("F25", "Conclusion", "Taken together, the chapter's security model stands, and the tests agree with it: a payment is safe to publish, the chain starts from one typed-in block, keys are better kept offline, backups need the descriptor and a recent date, and several keys or a delayed path protect against loss. What is old or missing lies at the edges: the statement on encryption of traffic, the single root of trust, the paper advice, the silence on secret sharing, the unnamed 7,000-bitcoin loss and the anchor [[ch11]] (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026).", ["P01", "P47"]),
 ("F26", "Comparative analysis", "The numbers of the chapter's security advice can be recomputed from whole-number inputs that a reader may change, and sixteen worked examples do so: the doubling PIN waits sum to 32,767 seconds, 9.1 hours, before the sixteenth attempt (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026); 12 words are 128 bits of entropy plus a 4-bit checksum, so a random wrong phrase passes with a chance of 1 in 16 and 1 in 256 for 24 words (Palatinus et al., 2013); a 2-of-3 scheme with keys lost at 10 percent is stuck at 0.028 and robbed at 0.00725 with keys stolen at 5 percent, and its spend weighs 146 virtual bytes counted field by field, as measured on the test chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); a (3, 5) threshold lets 16 groups of holders open a secret and 10 groups of two learn nothing (Shamir, 1979); and a relative wait of 10 is 100 minutes in blocks and 85 in units of 512 seconds, with 65,535 as the largest and 65,536 reading as 0 (Friedenbach et al., 2015).", ["P64", "P12", "P01", "P52", "P16"]),
 ("F27", "Conclusion", "The worked examples carry their assumptions with them and do not claim more than their sources: the loss chance of 10 percent, the theft chance of 5 percent and the rate of a trillion guesses a second are assumptions and not measurements, independent keys are a lower bound for keys that sit in one drawer, the 25,000-round stretch is timed on a 1.86 GHz Pentium M of the source and not on current hardware (Bitcoin Core, 2026), the 146 virtual bytes and the backup range are single-run values of the test chain, and the share of Mt. Gox and the 36 percent of Bitfinex are the figures of one article each (Wikipedia contributors, 2026); the Bitcoin Wiki gives a different count for Mt. Gox (Bitcoin Wiki, 2026).", ["P30", "P71", "P63"]),
]
